                         Vendik's Tale - Part 3
                           [Thy will be done]


    Outfitted now in blessed scale mail from Possogronoe's, I moved
more confidently through the twisty passages. As I descended further
and further into the depths of the ruin with Virgil at my side, I
successfully defeated hordes of reptiles and jackals.
    Moving northward down a narrow path, I came to a closed door.
My heart was racing as I tried the knob. Locked. I drew back and
ran toward the door, kicking it with all my might. On the second try
it burst open and I found myself in a temple. A great stone was
set up in the center of the room as an altar to the god to whom
the sight was sacred. But which god?
    A sense of peace enveloped me as I saw the priestess of Thoth.
Now, I thought, I might finally repay my god for his aid on my quest.
For indeed, Thoth had come to my aid not a day before, when, fainting
from hunger, I sent up my prayer. Thoth had answered, sparing me from
even considering my only other food alternative, my master's cat.
    I spoke with the priestess, and gave her a small donation for
the maintenance of the temple, receiving in return a blessing of
clairvoyance. My senses thus heightened, I stepped out of the temple
and felt the presence of a group of orcs in the next room. Virgil
and I slew them with little effort, and I hefted the corpses over my
shoulder and ran back to the temple. Leaning over the altar of Thoth,
I raised up the corpses, offering them to my god. Fire shot forth
from the altar, consuming the dead orcs, and I was vouchsafed a
vision of a clover, which I took to be a sign of fortune.
    Thinking to continue to honor my deity, I gathered up Virgil and
we slunk through the caves. A dwarf rose up against us, and after
a hard fight which brought me near death, we had another body to
sacrifice. I stripped the body of its cloak, shoes, and pick-axe,
and returned to the temple.
    Virgil and I continued our jihad for hours, returning each time
to the temple to offer thanks to Thoth. As we raised up the last
corpse, a fiery blade appeared on the altar in its place, and the
priestess spoke:
    "Oh blessed ones! Thoth has seen fit to grant you the mythical
firebrand, that you may prevail over your foes and do honor to his
name."
    Grinning at Virgil, I lifted the blade. To my surprise, it
was light, far more so than I would have expected from such a
great sword. I swung it a few times, exulting in the heat which
rose of its flaming blade. I looked again at the priestess.
    "Thank you, holy priestess," I murmured.
    "Thank only Thoth, supplicant," she replied, "and, should you
have found items you fear to be cursed, cast them down upon the
altar of your god."
    Surprised, I did as she said, laying each thing I carried
down on the altar. A black flash as I lay down two scrolls I had
found warned my that they were protected by curses, but, to my
joy, nothing else I carried was, and one of the potions actually
glowed a delicate amber on the altar! I snatched it up and drank.
    Hey, I thought, this is good. Kind of like that stuff the
master used to get...what was that? Wine, wine, of couse, wine.
Winewinewine. Boy, I do feel great. Just a little fuzzy, that's all.
Look! It's another orc. Well, I'll show that pig-headed fool
not to mess with a Wizard!
    I stumbled toward the orc, tripped, and fell, just missing
Virgil, who gave me a disapproving glance. As the orc came closer,
Virgil engaged it, leaving it dead and eating the corpse. I grinned
merrily at Virgil and picked up the scroll the orc had been
carrying. "Kirje, eh?" I slurred, "Ok, let's see what you can do."
I read the scroll through quickly, but in my drunken state mispronounced
some of the words. A golden glow surrounded my scale mail, and,
running my hands over it, it seemed a waxy film had settled
onto it.
    As my head cleared, leaving me with an awful headache, I looked
over at Virgil. Well, I thought, I guess it's time to keep going.
I cast a silent prayer of thanks up to Thoth for protecting me,
and followed Virgil to another staircase, and another layer
of caves.



